Be3 Nc6 9. Qf2 Be7 10. h4 O-O 11. O-O-O f5 12. Kb1 f4 13. Bc1 Bxf3 14. Qxf3
Ncxd4 15. Qxd5 Qxd5 16. Nxd5 Bc5 17. c3 Nc6 18. Bc4 Kh8 19. Rde1 Bf2 20. Re2
Bg3 21. Ne7 Rae8 22. Nxc6 bxc6 23. Bd2 g6 24. Rf1 Ng7 25. e6 Nf5 26. Rxf4 Bxf4
27. Bxf4 Ng7 28. Bxc7 h5 29. Be5 Kh7 30. Rd2 Nxe6 31. Rd7+ Kh6 32. g3 Rf5 33.
Bd6 Nc5 34. Rf7 Re1+ 35. Kc2 Rxf7 36. Bxf7 Ne4 37. Bf4+ Kg7 38. Bc4 Rg1 39. Be3
Rxg3 40. Bxa7 g5 41. Bd4+ Kg6 42. hxg5 h4 43. Be6 h3 44. Bxh3 Rxh3 45. b4 Kxg5
46. a4 Kf5 47. a5 Kg6 $4 (47... Nd6 {wins}) 48. a6 Nd6 $4 (48... Rf3 {still
draws}) 49. a7 Rh2+ 50. Kb3 c5 51. bxc5 {Sources: The Chess Amateur September
1913, p365. "The second prize fell to J. Mahood, of Lon­don. Candidly
speaking, no one expected Mr. Mahood, who was playing for the first time, to
gain such high honours. His success was due to an enterprising style of play,
and to some measure also to a splendid physical condition, as Mr. Mahood came
to the tournament straight from a fortnight's training with his regiment among
the Welsh hills. Mr. J. H. Blackburne was third, and but for some very
fortunate accidents which benefited Mahood, Blackburne would have been second.
(Gunsberg, Manchester Guardian, 24 Aug 1913); Belfast News-Letter, 20 November
1913} 1-0

